s a large financial edge: roughly $1.0 million on hand versus $277,000 for Tony Kozycki", "A lone Democratic-sponsored August poll shows a closer 46-43 race, but it is the only polling available", "Prediction markets price McCormick's win probability around 94 percent"], "rationale": "The district's strong Republican lean, McCormick's roughly four-to-one cash advantage, and market pricing near 94 percent all point to an incumbent win. The single late-August poll showing a 46-43 race suggests Kozycki keeps the margin competitive, so the expected margin sits in the high single digits rather than deep into safe territory.", "watch": "Any additional independent polling confirming or refuting the single-digit margin, or a sharp late move in the national environment, would be the most likely trigger for revision.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "Incumbent Rich McCormick (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican incumbent Rich McCormick seeks re-election in Georgia's 7th District, a seat that leans Republican by roughly eleven points. He faces Democrat Tony Kozycki, who emerged from a June runoff. McCormick holds a clear financial edge, having raised about $1.6 million with $1 million on hand, while Kozycki has raised roughly $393,000.\n\nA single late-August poll, conducted by a Democratic-affiliated firm, showed McCormick ahead 46 to 43 among likely voters, closer than the district's partisan lean would suggest. Prediction markets still price McCormick's chances near 94 percent. Campaign headlines have been largely routine: a symbolic bill, a debate challenge from Kozycki, and comments condemned by a civil rights group, none rising to a major scandal. The forecast expects McCormick to win by high single digits, with Kozycki needing an unusually strong national environment to close the gap.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0501, "margin": -12.18}, {"date": "2026-09-23"